    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the topic.\nCache-aside pattern should be understood in terms of state ownership, update frequency, consistency, caching/synchronization, testing, performance, and operational trade-offs.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method.\nStart with the smallest valid example. Identify the state owner, input/output contract, and one failure or edge path before optimizing.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\n```ts\ntype State = {\n  value: number;\n};\n\nfunction update(state: State): State {\n  return {\n    value: state.value + 1,\n  };\n}\n\nconsole.log(update({ value: 0 }));\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / PRACTICAL React/Redux TypeScript:\n```tsx\nconst next = {\n  value: state.value + 1,\n};\n\nconsole.log(next);\n```\n\nStep 5 — Example result:\n```text\n{ value: 1 }\n```\n\nStep 6 — Complexity / trade-off.\nPrefer the simplest mechanism that matches the state scope and lifecycle; avoid adding a state library where local state is sufficient.\n\nStep 7 — Edge cases.\nCheck empty/invalid input, duplicate requests, stale state, cleanup/cancellation, serialization, accessibility, and large-data behavior where relevant.\n\nStep 8 — Senior interview takeaway.\nExplain the mechanism, ownership boundary, failure mode, testing strategy, performance implications, and the production reason to choose this approach over alternatives.",
